llama.cpp (as fetched by Ollama v0.35.1, llama.cpp b11232, applied after
118): the router-weighted sum of the expert outputs (moe_weighted_reduction)
loads all of a token's expert rows before it accumulates, four columns per
thread.
Generic (all GPUs): not gfx803-specific code; measured on gfx803.

WHY
---
For a prompt batch, every MoE layer sums the outputs of the routed experts
per token, weighted by the router: with Qwen3.6 35B-A3B (8 experts used,
hidden size 2048) and a 2048-token batch that reads 134 MB and writes
16 MB per layer. The kernel gave each thread one column and walked the
expert rows in a loop whose length is only known at run time, one load and
one multiply-add per row, so each wave waited a full memory latency per
row. On an RX 590 it ran at about 37 GB/s: 4.04 ms per call, 40 calls and
0.16 s per 2048-token batch (rocprof medians), about 3.5 % of the batch.

WHAT
----
A second kernel, used when the layer routes 8 experts per token and the
expert, output and row strides allow 16-byte access, gives each thread four
adjacent columns of one token and issues all eight float4 row loads before
the first multiply. Every column still computes the scalar kernel's
expressions in the same order: (x * scale) * weight for the first expert,
then sum += (x * scale) * weight per further expert. On gfx803 both kernels
compile each term to the same v_mul_f32 followed by v_mac_f32, so the
result is bit-identical. Other expert counts and unaligned tensors keep the
scalar kernel.

Measured on an RX 590 (Qwen3.6 35B-A3B Q4_K_M, experts in system RAM,
llama-bench -ncmoe 99, 2048-token batches): 4.04 -> 1.17 ms per call
(rocprof medians), prompt processing 451 -> 463 and 442 -> 453 tok/s in
two alternating rounds (+2.6 %). llama-perplexity at 512-token batches
gives the same per-chunk perplexity, KL divergence and top-token agreement
as a run without the patch, line for line.

diff --git a/ggml/src/ggml-cuda/moe-weighted-reduction.cu b/ggml/src/ggml-cuda/moe-weighted-reduction.cu
--- a/ggml/src/ggml-cuda/moe-weighted-reduction.cu
+++ b/ggml/src/ggml-cuda/moe-weighted-reduction.cu
@@ -24,6 +24,49 @@
     dst[token * n_embd + col] = sum;
 }
 
+// One thread owns four adjacent columns of one token. All n_expert_used rows are loaded before the
+// first multiply so the loads overlap instead of each waiting for the previous accumulate; each
+// column still sums its experts in the same order and with the same expressions as the scalar kernel.
+template <int n_expert_used, bool has_scale>
+static __global__ void moe_weighted_reduction_f32_x4(const float * __restrict__ experts,
+                                                     const float * __restrict__ expert_scale,
+                                                     const float * __restrict__ weights,
+                                                     float * __restrict__ dst,
+                                                     const int64_t n_embd) {
+    const int64_t token = blockIdx.x;
+    const int64_t col   = 4 * ((int64_t) blockIdx.y * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x);
+    if (col >= n_embd) {
+        return;
+    }
+
+    const uint64_t first_row = (uint64_t) token * n_expert_used;
+    float4 x[n_expert_used];
+#pragma unroll
+    for (int e = 0; e < n_expert_used; ++e) {
+        x[e] = *(const float4 *) (experts + (first_row + e) * n_embd + col);
+    }
+
+    float4 sum;
+#pragma unroll
+    for (int e = 0; e < n_expert_used; ++e) {
+        const uint64_t row   = first_row + e;
+        const float    scale = has_scale ? expert_scale[row] : 1.0f;
+        const float    w     = weights[row];
+        if (e == 0) {
+            sum.x = (x[e].x * scale) * w;
+            sum.y = (x[e].y * scale) * w;
+            sum.z = (x[e].z * scale) * w;
+            sum.w = (x[e].w * scale) * w;
+        } else {
+            sum.x += (x[e].x * scale) * w;
+            sum.y += (x[e].y * scale) * w;
+            sum.z += (x[e].z * scale) * w;
+            sum.w += (x[e].w * scale) * w;
+        }
+    }
+    *(float4 *) (dst + token * n_embd + col) = sum;
+}
+
 static void launch_moe_weighted_reduction(const float * experts,
                                           const float * expert_scale,
                                           const float * weights,
@@ -33,6 +76,16 @@
                                           int           n_expert_used,
                                           cudaStream_t  stream) {
     constexpr int threads = 256;
+    const bool aligned = n_embd % 4 == 0 && (uintptr_t) experts % 16 == 0 && (uintptr_t) dst % 16 == 0;
+    if (n_expert_used == 8 && aligned) {
+        const dim3 blocks_x4(n_tokens, (n_embd / 4 + threads - 1) / threads, 1);
+        if (expert_scale != nullptr) {
+            moe_weighted_reduction_f32_x4<8, true><<<blocks_x4, threads, 0, stream>>>(experts, expert_scale, weights, dst, n_embd);
+        } else {
+            moe_weighted_reduction_f32_x4<8, false><<<blocks_x4, threads, 0, stream>>>(experts, expert_scale, weights, dst, n_embd);
+        }
+        return;
+    }
     const dim3 blocks(n_tokens, (n_embd + threads - 1) / threads, 1);
     moe_weighted_reduction_f32
         <<<blocks, threads, 0, stream>>>(experts, expert_scale, weights, dst, n_embd, n_expert_used);
